  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nCurrently, strong to moderate zonal flow aloft over the \nNorthern Plains with broad ridging from the Central Plains back\nnorthwest across the Rockies. At the surface, a ridge of high \npressure extends from southern Saskatchewan southeast into \nwestern Minnesota. A few light showers continue across the \nsouthern James River Valley into southeastern areas of the \nstate, associated with an area of low/mid level frontogensis. \n\nModels continue to suggest fog redeveloping into parts of\nsouthwest and south central ND later tonight, but have trended\nsouth with the aerial placement last few runs. Will maintain in\nthe forecast for now.  \n\nBroad ridging aloft amplifies and develops east for the \nupcoming weekend, trending temperatures warmer with highs in the\nmid 50s to mid 60s Sat and Sun. At the same time, boundary \nlayer moisture increases thanks to a southerly mid level flow\ntransporting 50+ degree Td values. With embedded disturbances \nmoving through the flow aloft, sky cover will be robust along\nwith a chances for light showers/drizzle both days.\n\nDeep upper level low pressure off the West Coast within long\nwave troughing aloft will swing inland Sunday, then crosses the\nRockies and moves into the Great Plains Sunday night into\nMonday. Ensembles and NBM continue to favor medium to high \nchances for rain west Sunday night and area wide for Monday/Mon\nnight, with precipitation chances slowly decreasing Tuesday \nfrom west to east as the main forcing departs to our east. Might\nsee chances linger longer on Tuesday depending the degree of \nwrap around that occurs. QPF amounts are forecast to range from \naround a quarter inch, with some area up to half an inch \npossible. Precip type will be all liquid with this event. \n\nThereafter, ensembles suggest lots of uncertainty after Tuesday,\nwith near normal temperatures expected for now in the 40s and \n50s and lows cooling to subfreezing by the middle of next week. \nPrecip chances will be heavily dependent on any upper level \nfeature moving through the region, and right now the forecast is\ndry both Wed and Thu.",
